<task type="tracer">
  <name>Task 1: Tracer - a raw header record becomes an identity-keyed ParsedMessage through the shared contracts</name>
  <reversibility rating="one-way">The pm:/mid:/hdr: key derivation and D-13 normalisation produce the stored dedup key; changing them later means rekeying every message (D-12). The owner locked the scheme in D-12/D-13, and the spike confirms the hash inputs, so no checkpoint re-asks.</reversibility>
  <files>apps/worker/src/ingest/types.ts, apps/worker/src/ingest/identity.ts, apps/worker/src/ingest/message.ts, apps/worker/test/ingest-identity.test.ts</files>
  <read_first>
    - .planning/phases/02-bridge-spike-and-imap-ingest/02-RESEARCH.md "Code Examples" (identity key, header request set), Pitfalls 7 and 12, Pre-spike evidence table (X-Pm-Internal-Id overwritten by Bridge, synthesised `@protonmail.internalid` Message-IDs)
    - .planning/phases/02-bridge-spike-and-imap-ingest/02-CONTEXT.md D-12, D-13, D-14
    - apps/worker/src/runtime/backoff.ts (pure-function style with decision-cited doc comments)
    - node_modules/libmime type declarations (decodeHeaders, decodeWords signatures)
  </read_first>
  <action>
1. `types.ts`: copy the interfaces block exactly. It holds only types and interfaces, so it is erasable-syntax clean.
2. `identity.ts`: implement the interfaces block.
   - normaliseMessageId: strip NUL, trim, remove one leading `<` and one trailing `>`, trim again, return null if empty, and lowercase only the substring after the last `@`.
   - stableHeaderHash: canonical JSON of `[name, values]` pairs for HDR_HASH_INPUTS in that order. Values are NUL-stripped, trimmed and internal whitespace collapsed to one space. Append the size, then sha256 hex via node:crypto.
   - identityKey: when trustPmHeader is true and pmInternalId, trimmed, matches `^[A-Za-z0-9_=-]{1,200}$`, return `pm:<id>`. Else a non-null normalised messageId gives `mid:<value>`. Else `hdr:<stableHash>`.
   - Doc comments cite D-12/D-13, Pitfall 12 (pm: only where Bridge overwrites the header, i.e. trustPmHeader) and A6 (hash inputs pending spike confirmation).
3. `message.ts`, header path only in this task: `HEADER_FIELDS`, the caps, `parseHeaderBlock` and `parseMessage`.
   - parseHeaderBlock uses libmime `decodeHeaders(raw.toString('binary' or 'utf8' per libmime docs))` and `decodeWords` per value. Header names are lowercased; NUL is stripped; each value is capped with truncateCodePoints(HEADER_VALUE_MAX_CHARS); at most HEADER_VALUES_MAX values per name.
   - parseMessage takes messageIdHeader from the raw `message-id` header (first value, normalised), never from the envelope. The raw bytes matter for D-13.
     - pmInternalId comes from `x-pm-internal-id`.
     - fromAddress comes from envelope.from[0].address with only the domain lowercased; fromDomain is that domain.
     - subject comes from envelope.subject, falling back to the decoded header, capped.
     - sentAt is envelope.date when it is a valid Date.
     - headers is the parsed header record; sizeBytes is rec.size; internalDate is rec.internalDate.
     - textPart and attachments come from Task 2 (for now return null and []).
4. `ingest-identity.test.ts`:
   - Synthetic header blocks only: never real email, and the `example.test` domain.
   - pm: wins when trusted and well-formed. A sender-forged `X-Pm-Internal-Id` on a non-trusted mailbox gives mid:. A malformed pm id falls back to mid:.
   - `<A.B@Example.TEST>` gives `mid:A.B@example.test`. `<A@x.test>` and `<a@x.test>` differ.
   - '', '   ' and '<>' give null and then hdr:. The hdr: hash is stable for equal input and differs when the Subject or the size differs.
   - A message with Bridge's synthesised `<id@protonmail.internalid>` Message-ID keys as pm: when trusted.
  </action>
  <verify>
    <automated>pnpm vitest run apps/worker/test/ingest-identity.test.ts && pnpm typecheck</automated>
